       "label": "Full script (clean copy, ~5 min)",
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "[COLD OPEN]\nNational contracts just fell almost seventeen percent. Pennsylvania sales fell nine and a half. Monroe County's numbers tell a different story, and it's not the one you'd guess.\n\n[INTRO]\nI'm Alexa Sanchez, a Realtor in the Poconos. Every week I go through the numbers. This week I want to put Monroe County next to the state and the country, so you can tell which headlines actually describe us.\n\n[PART 1 — RATES]\nStart with rates, because they drive everything else. Freddie Mac's 30-year average was 7.28% on November 19. A week earlier it was 7.11. A year ago, 6.26.\n\nHere's what that does to a buyer. On a $280,000 loan, the principal and interest is about $1,726 a month at last year's rate and about $1,916 now. That's about $190 more. Or flip it around: the payment that bought a $280,000 loan a year ago buys about $252,000 today. Your buyer's budget shrank by roughly $28,000 without them earning a dollar less.\n\n[PART 2 — THE COUNTRY]\nNationally, the National Association of REALTORS® says pending home sales, meaning signed contracts, fell 16.7% in October compared with last year. NAR's economist tied it to the rate jump from late August into October.\n\n[PART 3 — THE STATE]\nPennsylvania's latest full report is September: about 10,260 sales, down roughly 9.5% from a year earlier. But the statewide median price was $315,000, up about 6%.\n\n[PART 4 — MONROE COUNTY]\nNow us. In September, Monroe County closings fell 18.3%, about twice the state's decline. October closings fell 18.8%, to 203. So we slowed earlier and harder in closings.\n\nBut two things held up. The median sale price in October was $350,000, and contracts didn't fall nearly as hard as the nation's: Monroe's October pending sales were 201, down 3.8%. That came after bigger drops in July, August and September.\n\nOne month isn't a trend. I'm not calling a bottom. I'm saying the national headline isn't our headline this month.\n\n[PART 5 — WHAT IT MEANS FOR YOU]\nIf you're selling this winter: price for this year's buyer, the one with the smaller budget, and against what's listed near you now. Monroe had 923 homes for sale in October, about 4.6% more than last year.\n\nIf you're buying: rates are a national average, not your quote. Get more than one.\n\nIf you're watching: November's pending number is the one to wait for. It'll tell us whether October's small dip held.\n\n[CLOSE]\nIf you want to know where your home fits in this market, my contact information is in the description. I'll look at what's sold and what's listed near you and give you a straight answer.\n\nSources: Freddie Mac, the National Association of REALTORS®, the Pennsylvania Association of REALTORS®, and the Pocono Mountains Association of REALTORS®. See you next week."
